<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Coimbra</h1><p class="st-meta">878 CE – 987 CE (approx.) · capital Coimbra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A frontier county of the Kingdom of Asturias and León created when Christian forces took Coimbra in 878, lost to Almanzor in 987.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermenegildo-gutierrez">Hermenegildo Gutiérrez</a>, a noble serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III of Asturias</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mondego">Mondego</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a>. The city became the centre of a frontier county stretching south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a>, settled by northern Christians and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarabs</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts were military governors for the Asturian and Leonese kings, defending a vulnerable frontier and organising resettlement. Mozarabic Christians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> brought Arabic culture and Visigothic church traditions, and monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorvao-monastery">Lorvão</a> became important landowners. Power passed among a few noble families who intermarried with the counts of Portugal.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 987 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>, ending the county; the city was left largely deserted for a time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-leon">Ferdinand I of León</a> retook it in 1064, and in 1096 it was joined to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-portugal">County of Portugal</a>, later becoming the first capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 878 CE – 987 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.17%;width:2.17%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>878 CE – 987 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Coimbra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hermenegildo Gutiérrez</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Condado de Coimbra</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=932&amp;focus=county-of-coimbra" aria-label="County of Coimbra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4473 740 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Coimbra lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4793 996L4859 895M4793 996L4865 919M4793 996L4881 1071"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4859 895h0M4865 919h0M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4793 996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4793 996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Coimbra. <a href="/atlas?year=932&amp;focus=county-of-coimbra">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

